# Plan: Server-Verified FIPS Source Checksums for postWolf

> **Amendment — Issue #1 from `README-fips-issues.md`:** This plan was
> originally written assuming the MTC server's existing single Ed25519
> CA key would also sign FIPS manifest tree roots. That conflated two
> roles. The amended design splits the keys:
>
> - **Log key** — online, on the MTC server, signs every tree root.
> - **CA key** — offline, on a separate (ideally air-gapped) machine,
>   signs the log key certificate plus periodic checkpoints. Verifiers
>   pin the CA key, never the log key.
>
> Wherever this plan says "the CA key signs the tree root" or
> "the same Ed25519 key signs manifests," the intended behaviour is now
> "the log key signs every tree root in real time; the CA key
> co-signs periodic checkpoints offline." See
> `README-fips.md` §"The Solution" for the rewritten architecture.
> Implementation impact is itemized inline below with `[ISSUE #1]` tags.

> **Amendment — Issue #2 from `README-fips-issues.md`:** Pinning of the
> CA public key is **mandatory**, not optional. The verifier MUST read
> the CA key from a local pinned file
> (`/etc/postWolf/ca-pubkey/<name>.der` system-wide,
> `~/.config/mtc-fips/ca-pubkey/<name>.der` per-user, or
> `fips-framework/config/ca-pubkey.h` compiled-in). Receipts not
> chaining to a pinned key MUST fail closed — no trust-on-first-use,
> no fallback to network/website/receipt-embedded keys. The bootstrap
> channels (DNS TXT, project website, package metadata, GPG-signed
> tag, CMVP cert) are for first-time acquisition and key-change
> detection only, never for verification-time trust. The receipt
> ships *inside* the tarball and is therefore not self-validating —
> the pinned CA key, sourced from a channel the attacker does not
> control, is the only real defence. Implementation tags `[ISSUE #2]`.

> **Amendment — postWolf TODO #74 phase 2 + 3 (server-side
> storage refactor, 2026-05-04 / 2026-05-07):** This plan was
> originally written against the in-memory `MtcMerkleTree`
> (`mtc_merkle.c`) and the file-mode persistence path
> (`mtc_store_save` writing `entries.json` /
> `certificates.json` / `revocations.json`).  Both are gone:
>
> - The Merkle tree is now a **tiled tree** (`MtcTiledTree`,
>   `mtc_merkle_tiled.{c,h}`) backed by Neon
>   (`mtc_merkle_tiles` + `mtc_merkle_top_nodes`) with bounded
>   per-process LRUs in front.  The `mtc_tiled_tree_*` API
>   exposes the same algorithmic surface as the legacy
>   `mtc_tree_*` API (`_root_hash`, `_subtree_hash`,
>   `_inclusion_proof`, `_consistency_proof`,
>   `_append_leaf`).  The legacy `mtc_merkle.c` stays in the
>   tree only for `mtc_rebuild_tiles`'s migration walk; new
>   callers must NOT use it.
> - `mtc_store_save`, `_load`, `_reload`, the `entries.json`
>   / `certificates.json` / `revocations.json` mirrors, and
>   the `reload_thread` + `reload_lock` plumbing are
>   **retired**.  `MERKLE_NEON` is mandatory.
> - `mtc_store_add_entry` now opens a Postgres transaction,
>   takes `pg_advisory_xact_lock(FNV-1a-64(log_id))`, calls
>   `mtc_db_save_entry` + `mtc_tiled_tree_append_leaf`, and
>   commits.  Concurrent FIPS-manifest submissions
>   serialise cleanly without any new code in the FIPS
>   handler.
>
> Inline impact below is itemized with `[PH3]` tags.  Wherever
> this plan says `mtc_tree_*`, `mtc_merkle.c`,
> `mtc_store_save`, or "file-based fallback", read the `[PH3]`
> note for the post-phase-3 form.
>
> The wire-format from a FIPS verifier's point of view is
> unchanged: the receipt JSON still carries a leaf type byte
> (`0x02`), the canonical manifest, an inclusion proof, and a
> log signature + checkpoint cosignature.  Phase 3 is a
> server-side storage detail.

> **Amendment — Issue #10 from `README-fips-issues.md`:** All references
> below to "Ed25519" describe the original design and have been (or
> are being) swept to **ML-DSA-87** (FIPS 204). Concrete impact:
>
> - `mtc_store_cosign()` (Ed25519, 64-byte sig, 32-byte pubkey) is
>   replaced by `mtc_store_log_sign()` and `mtc_ca_sign_checkpoint()`
>   per `[ISSUE #1]`, both ML-DSA-87 (4627-byte sig, 2592-byte
>   pubkey, 4896-byte private key).
> - `wc_ed25519_verify_msg()` is replaced by
>   `wc_dilithium_verify_ctx_msg()` (the wolfCrypt API name retains
>   "dilithium"; the parameter set is standardized ML-DSA-87).
> - `config/ca-pubkey.h` carries 2592 bytes, not 32.
> - DNS TXT records cannot inline a 2592-byte pubkey verbatim and
>   must use the `pkhash=sha256:...` indirection plus a side-channel
>   pubkey fetch (see `README-fips.md` §"Verifying a Kit End-to-End"
>   Step 2).
> - Receipt sizes grow accordingly; update Architecture Diagram and
>   What-Each-Layer-Proves tables. Implementation tags `[ISSUE #10]`.

## Context

OpenSSL's FIPS integrity system stores SHA256 checksums of source files in the same repository as the source. An attacker with write access can modify both a source file and its checksum — nothing downstream detects it. The HMAC key is public, so rebuilding and re-running `fipsinstall` produces valid MACs for the tampered binary.

postWolf has the same weakness in `fips-hash.sh` / `fips_test.c`. However, postWolf already has a **Merkle Tree Certificate (MTC) transparency log** with ML-DSA-87 cosignatures (originally Ed25519; migrated as part of this work — `[ISSUE #10]`), inclusion proofs, and a REST API — implemented as a **C server** (`mtc-keymaster/server2/c/`). We can leverage this infrastructure to anchor FIPS source checksums in the append-only server-side log, making tampering detectable.

**Goal:** At build time, log a signed manifest of FIPS source file checksums to the C MTC server. At verification time, any party can check local source files against the server-logged manifest using inclusion proofs and ML-DSA-87 signatures — with or without network access.

## Approach

### What Gets Logged

A **FIPS Build Manifest** — a canonical JSON document listing every FIPS source file and its SHA256 hash. The SHA256 of this manifest becomes a leaf in the Merkle tree via `mtc_store_add_entry()` → `mtc_tiled_tree_append_leaf()` (`[PH3]`; legacy `mtc_tree_append` is gone).

Entry type byte: `0x02` (new — existing types are `0x00` null, `0x01` TBS certificate).

```json
{
  "type": "fips-build-manifest",
  "version": 1,
  "package": "postWolf",
  "git_commit": "abc123...",
  "git_tag": "v5.9.0",
  "timestamp": 1712188800.0,
  "expires": "2027-04-05T00:00:00Z",
  "files": [
    {"path": "wolfcrypt/src/aes.c", "sha256": "0e22ea..."},
    {"path": "wolfcrypt/src/fips.c", "sha256": "c049a9..."}
  ]
}
```

Files array sorted by `path`. Canonical form uses json-c with sorted keys and no extra whitespace — matching the deterministic serialization used for TBS entries in `mtc_http.c:481-503`.

### Phase 1: C Server Extensions

**File: `mtc-keymaster/server2/c/mtc_http.c`**

Add endpoint handlers following the `handle_certificate_request()` pattern (lines 395-593):

- `handle_fips_manifest_submit()` — `POST /fips/manifest`
  1. Parse JSON body with `json_tokener_parse()`
  2. Validate required fields: `package`, `git_commit`, `expires`, `files[]` with `path` + `sha256`
  3. Build canonical JSON string (sorted keys via `json_object_object_add()` in sorted order)
  4. Prefix with `0x02` byte, append canonical JSON → entry buffer
  5. Call `mtc_store_add_entry(store, entry_buf, entry_sz)` → returns index.  `[PH3]` This now opens a Postgres transaction, takes `pg_advisory_xact_lock(FNV-1a-64(log_id))`, calls `mtc_db_save_entry` + `mtc_tiled_tree_append_leaf`, and commits — no caller-side locking required.
  6. `[PH3]` Call `mtc_tiled_tree_inclusion_proof(&store->tree, idx, 0, store->tree.size, &proof, &proof_count)` for the proof; the legacy `mtc_tree_*` API is gone.
  7. `[PH3]` Call `mtc_tiled_tree_subtree_hash(&store->tree, 0, store->tree.size, &subtree_hash)` + `mtc_store_log_sign()` for the ML-DSA-87 log signature, then await an `mtc_ca_sign_checkpoint()` covering this size from the offline CA host (`[ISSUE #1]` keys split, `[ISSUE #10]` algorithm sweep)
  8. Build response JSON with index, proof, log signature, checkpoint cosignature, subtree hash
  9. `[PH3]` Persist via `mtc_db_save_fips_manifest()` only — the tree state is already authoritative in Neon (`mtc_log_entries` + `mtc_merkle_tiles` + `mtc_merkle_top_nodes`) by step 5.  No `mtc_store_save()` call (file mode retired).
  10. Return 201 Created

- `handle_fips_manifest_get()` — `GET /fips/manifest/<index>`
  1. Extract index from path
  2. Validate entry exists and has type byte `0x02`
  3. Return stored manifest JSON

- `handle_fips_manifest_proof()` — `GET /fips/manifest/<index>/proof`
  1. `[PH3]` Call `mtc_tiled_tree_inclusion_proof(&store->tree, idx, 0, store->tree.size, ...)` with the current tree size
  2. Call `mtc_store_log_sign()` (or whatever roles `[ISSUE #1]` settles on) for fresh log signature; recall the latest `mtc_ca_sign_checkpoint` cosignature from `mtc_checkpoints` (`[ISSUE #1]`)
  3. Return proof + log signature + checkpoint cosignature

- `handle_fips_manifest_search()` — `GET /fips/manifest/search?package=X&tag=Y`
  1. Scan entries with type `0x02`, filter by metadata
  2. Return matching indices + summaries

Add routing in `handle_request()` (lines 901-1019):
```c
} else if (strcmp(method, "POST") == 0 && strcmp(path, "/fips/manifest") == 0) {
    handle_fips_manifest_submit(client_fd, store, body);
} else if (strcmp(method, "GET") == 0 && strncmp(path, "/fips/manifest/", 15) == 0) {
    if (strstr(path, "/proof"))
        handle_fips_manifest_proof(client_fd, store, path);
    else if (strstr(path, "search"))
        handle_fips_manifest_search(client_fd, store, query);
    else
        handle_fips_manifest_get(client_fd, store, path);
}
```

**File: `mtc-keymaster/server2/c/mtc_db.c`**

Add `mtc_fips_manifests` table to schema initialization (`mtc_db_init_schema()`):
```sql
CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS mtc_fips_manifests (
    index       INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,
    manifest    TEXT NOT NULL,
    package     TEXT,
    git_tag     TEXT,
    created_at  TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT now()
);
CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx_fips_package ON mtc_fips_manifests(package);
CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx_fips_tag ON mtc_fips_manifests(git_tag);
```

Add functions following `mtc_db_save_certificate()` / `mtc_db_load_certificates()` pattern:
- `mtc_db_save_fips_manifest(MtcDb *db, int index, const char *manifest_json, const char *package, const char *git_tag)`
- `mtc_db_search_fips_manifests(MtcDb *db, const char *package, const char *tag, ...)`

**File: `mtc-keymaster/server2/c/mtc_store.c`**

Add entry type detection for `0x02` in `mtc_store_add_entry()`:
```c
if (entrySz > 0 && entry[0] == 0x02) {
    entry_type = 2;  /* FIPS manifest */
    /* extract package/tag for DB indexing */
}
```

`[PH3]` No file-based fallback persistence — file mode is
retired and `MtcStore` no longer owns resident
`certificates[]` / `revoked_indices[]` / `fips_manifests[]`
arrays.  The submit handler calls `mtc_db_save_fips_manifest`
directly; subsequent lookups re-fault from Neon (mirror the
`mtc_cert_cache` LRU pattern from phase 2 if the lookup rate
warrants it).

**File: `mtc-keymaster/server2/c/mtc_store.h`**

`[PH3]` No new fields on `MtcStore`.  The phase-2/3 refactor
moved cert + revocation state out of the struct; FIPS
manifests follow the same pattern (Neon authoritative,
LRU-faulted on demand).

**No changes needed to `mtc_merkle_tiled.c`** —
`mtc_tiled_tree_append_leaf`, `_inclusion_proof`,
`_subtree_hash`, and the log/checkpoint signing helpers
(`mtc_store_log_sign` + `mtc_ca_sign_checkpoint` per
`[ISSUE #1]`) all operate on opaque leaf bytes including type
`0x02`.  `mtc_merkle.c` (legacy in-memory tree) is no longer
in any production code path; do not add new callers.

### Phase 2: Client Tooling (`fips-framework/`)

All client tooling lives in `fips-framework/`, a self-contained directory
that builds two C tools against wolfCrypt. No shell script dependencies
(`jq`, `curl`), no OpenSSL dependency.

```
fips-framework/
    README.md                     Documentation
    Makefile                      Builds both tools against wolfCrypt
    fips-manifest-submit.c        Kit registration tool
    fips-manifest-verify.c        Kit verification tool
    config/
        ca-pubkey.h               Pinned CA ML-DSA-87 public key (2592 bytes — `[ISSUE #2]` mandatory pin source for compiled-in builds; mutually exclusive with the runtime-file pin paths)
```

**`fips-manifest-submit.c`** — run by publisher after building a kit:
1. Enumerate FIPS source files (from compiled-in file list or command-line args)
2. Compute SHA-256 of each file using `wc_Sha256Hash()`
3. Build canonical JSON manifest (sorted by path, includes `expires` — default 1 year)
4. HTTP POST manifest to `$MTC_SERVER/fips/manifest`
5. Save server response to `fips-manifest-receipt.json`

**`fips-manifest-verify.c`** — run by user to check a kit:

All verification uses the MTC Merkle tree and ML-DSA-87 cosignatures natively (`[ISSUE #10]`: was Ed25519 in the original plan; sweep complete).
The Merkle inclusion proof + cosignature replaces both `openssl35 verify` (cert
chain) and `openssl35 dgst -verify` (signature):

- Manifest hash → Merkle leaf (via `mtc_store_add_entry` → `mtc_tiled_tree_append_leaf`; `[PH3]`)
- Inclusion proof → binds leaf to tree root
- ML-DSA-87 log signature → log key signs the tree root in real time (via `mtc_store_log_sign` — new helper, see `[ISSUE #1]` below)
- ML-DSA-87 CA checkpoint → CA key signs the covering checkpoint offline (via `mtc_ca_sign_checkpoint` on the offline CA host; uploaded back to the log)

Verification steps:
- **Expiration check:** reject manifests past their `expires` timestamp
- **Rollback detection:** maintain `~/.config/mtc-fips/last-verified.json`; warn/fail if `git_tag` is older (override with `--allow-rollback`)
- **Publisher verification:** check the publisher leaf's log entry against the pinned CA public key
- **Online mode:** recompute local hashes → GET `/fips/manifest/<index>` → compare → GET `/fips/manifest/<index>/proof` → replay inclusion proof → verify log signature on the returned root → verify CA signature on the covering checkpoint with the pinned CA key
- **Offline mode:** recompute local hashes → replay inclusion proof from receipt → verify log signature → verify CA-signed checkpoint with pinned CA key from `config/ca-pubkey.h`. (Log signature alone is necessary but not sufficient; a verified covering CA checkpoint is required for full audit weight.)

ML-DSA-87 verification uses `wc_dilithium_verify_ctx_msg()` from wolfCrypt (the ML-DSA-87 wire algorithm is named "Dilithium" in wolfCrypt's API for historical reasons; the parameter set is the standardized FIPS 204 ML-DSA-87).
SHA-256 uses `wc_Sha256Hash()`. HTTP uses minimal socket code (no libcurl).

**What ships with the kit:**
- `fips-manifest-receipt.json` — manifest + inclusion proof + cosignature (self-contained)

### Phase 3: Build Integration

**File: `debian/rules`**
- In `override_dh_auto_build` (FIPS path): call `./fips-manifest-submit.sh` after `fips-hash.sh`'s second `make` pass

**File: `Makefile.am`**
- Add `fips-manifest-submit` and `fips-manifest-verify` targets

**Ship `fips-manifest-receipt.json`** with the package (add to `EXTRA_DIST`).
This single file contains the manifest, inclusion proof, log signature, and CA-signed checkpoint. **`[ISSUE #2]` It is not self-validating** — it ships inside the same tarball it certifies, so an attacker who substitutes the tarball can substitute the receipt. Verification's defence rests entirely on the pinned CA public key (2592 bytes, obtained out-of-band, never refreshed from the receipt or from the network at verify time). A receipt that does not chain to a pre-pinned CA key MUST be rejected; no first-use prompt, no auto-pin.

### Reused Existing C Code

| Component | Existing Code | Reuse |
|-----------|---------------|-------|
| Merkle tree | `[PH3]` `mtc_merkle_tiled.c`: `mtc_tiled_tree_append_leaf()`, `mtc_tiled_tree_inclusion_proof()`, `mtc_tiled_tree_subtree_hash()` (legacy `mtc_merkle.c` retired in production) | Direct — operates on opaque leaf bytes, type `0x02` included |
| ML-DSA-87 log signing | `mtc_store.c:539-583`: `mtc_store_cosign()` (originally Ed25519, migrated to ML-DSA-87 — `[ISSUE #10]`) | Renamed to `mtc_store_log_sign()` per `[ISSUE #1]` (online log key, signs every root). The CA-checkpoint cosignature lives in separate offline tooling. |
| Entry persistence | `mtc_store.c:469-503`: `mtc_store_add_entry()` | Direct — just add type 0x02 detection |
| DB persistence | `mtc_db.c`: `mtc_db_save_certificate()` pattern | Copy for `mtc_db_save_fips_manifest()` |
| HTTP routing | `mtc_http.c:901-1019`: `handle_request()` | Add new routes to existing dispatch |
| HTTP helpers | `mtc_http.c`: `http_send_json()`, `http_send_error()` | Direct |
| JSON handling | `mtc_http.c:395-593`: `handle_certificate_request()` | Pattern for `handle_fips_manifest_submit()` |
| File I/O | `mtc_store.c`: `write_file()`, `read_file()` | Direct for file-based fallback |
| Key management | `mtc_store.c:64-152`: CA key load/generate | **`[ISSUE #1]` Split required.** Existing single-key load/generate becomes two paths: `mtc_store_load_log_key()` (online, signs roots) and `mtc_ca_load_key()` (offline-only tooling, signs checkpoints + log certs; never linked into `mtc_server`). The MTC server binary must NOT be able to load a CA key — enforce by build-time symbol separation. |

